WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.172
In an Express request pipeline,

2
00:00:02.252 --> 00:00:06.835
authentication should establish trusted
identity before object authorization runs.

3
00:00:06.915 --> 00:00:11.126
Session middleware or token verification
can produce a stable internal subject

4
00:00:11.206 --> 00:00:15.418
identifier, tenant or organization
context, and trusted roles or permissions.

5
00:00:15.498 --> 00:00:19.647
Route code should not rebuild those facts
from a user ID, tenant ID, owner field,

6
00:00:19.727 --> 00:00:21.647
or role supplied in the request body.

7
00:00:21.727 --> 00:00:24.328
Client input describes the requested
operation;

8
00:00:24.408 --> 00:00:28.310
server-controlled authentication state
describes who is requesting it.

9
00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:32.601
A typed authentication context gives the
rest of the application one consistent

10
00:00:32.681 --> 00:00:33.300
interface.

11
00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.291
It can contain a user ID, tenant ID,
immutable role list,

12
00:00:36.371 --> 00:00:39.839
and other claims that the authentication
boundary has validated.

13
00:00:39.919 --> 00:00:44.192
Attach it to the request through a
deliberate Express type augmentation or pass it

14
00:00:44.272 --> 00:00:46.502
as a separate value into the service
layer.

15
00:00:46.582 --> 00:00:50.793
Avoid repeatedly casting a loosely typed
req dot user object in handlers because

16
00:00:50.873 --> 00:00:54.465
those casts can hide missing middleware,
inconsistent claim names,

17
00:00:54.545 --> 00:00:55.784
and unverified values.

18
00:00:55.864 --> 00:00:59.889
Validate route parameters and request
bodies before they reach data access.

19
00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:02.818
TypeScript can describe a route parameter
as a string,

20
00:01:02.898 --> 00:01:05.995
but it does not prove that the string has
an accepted format,

21
00:01:06.075 --> 00:01:08.738
that an object exists, or that the caller
may use it.

22
00:01:08.818 --> 00:01:13.091
Runtime validation should reject malformed
identifiers and unsupported action

23
00:01:13.171 --> 00:01:13.605
values.

24
00:01:13.685 --> 00:01:17.772
Authorization still runs after validation
because syntactic validity is not

25
00:01:17.852 --> 00:01:18.533
permission.

26
00:01:18.613 --> 00:01:21.462
Make security context visible in service
signatures.

27
00:01:21.542 --> 00:01:25.630
A function named updateProject that
accepts only a project ID and patch leaves

28
00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.072
authorization optional.

29
00:01:27.152 --> 00:01:29.939
A function that requires an AuthContext,
project ID,

30
00:01:30.019 --> 00:01:33.239
and validated command makes the dependency
harder to ignore.

31
00:01:33.319 --> 00:01:37.221
The service can then call a scoped
repository or explicit policy before the

32
00:01:37.301 --> 00:01:37.858
mutation.

33
00:01:37.938 --> 00:01:41.530
Keep the context narrow: pass trusted
facts needed for the decision,

34
00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:43.283
not the entire Express request.

35
00:01:43.363 --> 00:01:46.645
This separation improves tests, reduces
framework coupling,

36
00:01:46.725 --> 00:01:50.998
and keeps authorization behavior
consistent when the same service is called from

37
00:01:51.078 --> 00:01:53.060
HTTP routes, jobs, or other adapters.
